WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:05.760
Trying to stay healthy can seem&nbsp;
like a full-time job sometimes.&nbsp;

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:09.520
Especially with all the conflicting&nbsp;
information that’s out there.&nbsp;

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:13.440
But, I’m here to make that job a little easier.&nbsp;

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:18.720
Welcome to the Nutrition Facts podcast,
I’m your host Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:23.680
Did you know that excess consumption of
ultra-processed foods is linked to increased&nbsp;

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:25.360
risks of disease and death?&nbsp;

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:30.080
Today we explore the public health implications
of full or partial replacement of&nbsp;

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:37.600
animal products with both ultra-processed
plant-based products and whole plant foods.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:39.120
About a century ago

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:43.680
in the context of nutrient deficiency diseases.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:49.280
So, there were editorials with
titles like “Sugar as Food,”

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:52.880
heralding sugar as one of the
cheapest sources of calories.

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:57.520
For a mere six cents, you could
buy three thousand calories.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:00:58.560
What a bargain!

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:03.840
But the Nutrient Deficiency era
gave way to the Dietary Excess era.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:09.200
No longer were we dying of nutrient
deficiency diseases, like scurvy,

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:13.840
as much as we were dying from
nutrient excess diseases,

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:16.160
like obesity and heart disease.

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:19.760
So, it became more about
avoiding too many calories,

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:21.120
too much saturated fat,

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:23.120
too much sugar, too much sodium,

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:27.840
but either way still focused on nutrients.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:33.200
This allowed food companies
to get away with abominations

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:35.840
like frosting-filled cereal,

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:42.080
because it was fortified with
twelve vitamins and minerals,

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:49.200
50% better than the measly eight
in Marshmallow Froot Loops.

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:54.480
However, food, not nutrients, is
the fundamental unit in nutrition.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:56.240
And to their credit,

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:01.240
the field of nutrition started moving
toward a more holistic view itself.

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:06.480
So, first generation dietary guidelines
emphasized individual nutrients

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:10.880
moved on to second generation
food-based dietary guidelines,

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:18.240
which largely converged on encouraging
diets rich in vegetables, fruits,

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:20.960
legumes—which are beans, split
peas, chickpeas, and lentils—

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:22.640
whole grains and nuts,

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:26.400
but an area of emerging importance

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:30.560
was the degree of food processing.

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:33.520
What if, when it comes to nutrition and health,

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:35.200
it’s not so much the food or the nutrients

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:37.360
as much as the level of processing?

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:42.720
For instance, a food-based dietary
guideline might say something like

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:48.480
“Eat more vegetable soup.” Great!

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:53.280
But there’s vegetable soup, and
then there’s vegetable soup.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:57.200
Are we talking a
clean-out-the-fridge vegetable soup?

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:02.160
A health-haloed quinoa and kale organic

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:08.320
with a heart-stopping 1,200 milligrams of sodium?

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:15.200
How about a vegetable soup with
more salt than there are vegetables?

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:21.200
Or a vegetable flavor soup that
has more artificial colors and MSG

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:23.920
than it has vegetables.

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:25.760
All soup is not the same.

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:29.040
The degree of processing matters.

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:33.360
Ultra-processed foods are these
industrial formulations which,

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:36.400
besides salt, sugar, oils, and fats,

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:39.120
include substances that are
really not used in cooking,

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:42.640
like added ﬂavors, colors, artificial sweeteners,

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:45.840
emulsiﬁers, and other additives
used to imitate real foods,

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:48.480
like a frosted grape Pop Tart

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:51.680
with more grapes on the front of the package

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:54.320
than are actually in it,

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:56.640
with less grapes than there are salt,

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:02.320
but it may artificially taste like grapes

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:04.480
and look like grapes because of

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:09.920
one, two, three, four, five different food dyes.

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:13.200
Simply put, ultra-processed foods are foods

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:14.880
that can’t be made in a home kitchen,

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:18.160
because they have been chemically
or physically transformed

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:20.160
using industrial processes.

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:23.040
They typically contain little or no whole foods,

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:25.280
are ready-to-consume or heat up,

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:27.280
and are fatty, salty, or sugary

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:30.000
and depleted in dietary fiber and other nutrients.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:34.640
So, that’s like all the sweet, fatty, salty snacks

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:37.520
like potato chips, ice cream, soda, candy,

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:39.680
French fries, burgers, hot dogs,

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:41.040
chicken nuggets, fish sticks.

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:43.520
Basically, everything in a box or a bag.

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:46.880
Why not just call them “packaged foods”?

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:48.400
Well, they were actually thinking about it,

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:50.400
but they were afraid that some consumers

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:52.160
would look at like a bag of apples

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:55.520
and get confused or something.

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:59.200
But what is so revolutionary

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:02.240
about this concept of ultra-processed foods?

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:07.040
I mean, wasn’t fatty, salty,
sugary junk always a bad idea?

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:13.760
Why isn’t it enough to just tell
people to stay away from junk?

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:19.280
Because, Diet Coke.

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:21.280
Diet soda is the perfect example

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:26.400
of why a new kind of term like
ultra-processed can be so useful.

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:28.880
Because it has no calories.

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:32.320
No fat. No sugar. So, no problem, right?

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:37.200
Well then, why is diet soda consumption
associated with premature death,

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:39.200
stroke, heart disease, hypertension,

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:41.840
diabetes, metabolic syndrome, leukemia?

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:44.320
And most studies controlled for body weight;

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:49.840
so, it’s not just because heavier
people are more likely to drink it.

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:54.400
Health risks are not only related
to the nutritional quality

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:58.480
of ultra-processed foods but also
to the presence of additives.

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:02.480
No apparent calories, fat, sugar, or salt,

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:05.760
but contains caramel color,

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:09.040
which results in the formation
of 4-methylimidazole,

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:13.120
which is a potential human carcinogen.

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:15.840
And contains aspartame,

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:20.640
also recently classified as
possibly cancer-causing in humans

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:24.480
And contains phosphoric acid
which is a phosphate additive

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:27.040
that may be damaging our health as well.

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:29.840
And contains a benzoate preservative.

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:33.040
If you remove artificial colorings
and benzoate preservatives

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:34.560
from the diets of preschoolers

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:37.280
and then randomize them to be slipped a placebo

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:40.320
or a hidden cocktail of colors and benzoate,

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:43.120
there is a significant reduction
in hyperactive behavior

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:47.760
when they removed these compounds

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:50.320
and then a significant increase
in hyperactive behavior

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:52.000
when they got the colorings and benzoate

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:54.480
compared to getting the placebo.

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:56.080
Now, of course, it could have been the colors,

00:06:56.080 --> 00:06:58.560
not the benzoate, but that’s one of the problems.

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:03.840
As little as we know about the
effects of these individual additives,

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:07.600
we know even less what
combinations of them can do.

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:10.560
There is a large body of evidence

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:13.840
suggesting toxicity from certain food colors,

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:16.400
preservatives, sweeteners, and emulsifiers,

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:19.760
in part sometimes through our gut microbiome.

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:23.440
Now, industry apologists argue

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:27.120
that the FDA carefully evaluates food additives

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:30.640
to make sure that they are
safe, until they’re not.

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:33.120
In fact, to show the system works,

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:38.640
they cite the removal of six
carcinogenic artificial flavors in 2018.

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:41.920
Artificial flavors that had
been in the food supply,

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:45.840
approved for safety, since 1964.

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:50.400
So, we were exposed for more than
50 years before they were banned.

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:56.640
And that’s their example of the system working!

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:00.880
Aspartame, NutraSweet, was officially recognized

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:03.840
as a potential human carcinogen in 2023,

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:08.880
42 years after the FDA’s
own public board of inquiry

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:12.800
opposed approval based on it
causing brain tumors in animals

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:14.320
in the industry’s own studies.

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:20.160
This included several FDA scientists
who didn’t think it should be approved.

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:22.640
The FDA Commissioner, however,

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:25.600
rejected these concerns and approved it anyway,

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:30.800
before leaving the agency to enjoy
a $1,000 a day consultancy position

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:34.720
with the aspartame company’s PR firm.

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:39.360
And then, the FDA actually prevented
the National Toxicology Program

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:42.640
from doing further cancer testing.

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:47.040
Meanwhile, literally tens of millions of pounds

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:50.960
made its way into the food supply.

00:08:50.960 --> 00:08:56.800
Remember when the food industry thought
partially hydrogenated oils was a good idea?

00:08:56.800 --> 00:09:01.360
Let’s replace saturated fats with trans fats.

00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:04.080
Although many countries now restrict their use,

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:07.760
trans fats continue to kill an
estimated half-million people

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:09.120
around the world every year.

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:14.640
Of course, saturated fat is also probably
killing hundreds of thousands a year,

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:20.160
but the point is that trans fats from
partially hydrogenated vegetable oil

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:24.400
killed people for decades before
there were any limits on it.

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:27.920
The FDA didn’t ban it until 25 years

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:31.440
after the first solid evidence that it
increased the risk of heart disease,

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:34.960
and meanwhile, every single one of those years,

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:39.280
trans fats were killing an
estimated 50,000 Americans a year.

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:42.080
That is quite the death toll

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:47.440
that can be laid at the feet of
the ultra-processed food industry.

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:50.320
But they originally thought it was safe!

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:52.880
That’s the problem.

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:56.880
Any time a chemical company
comes up with a new preservative

00:09:56.880 --> 00:09:59.120
or sweetener or artificial color,

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:03.280
we have no idea how it is going
to turn out decades later.

00:10:03.280 --> 00:10:09.520
So, we can start to see the value of
this ultra-processed food concept,

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:12.800
in which an entire category of products

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:16.560
is essentially presumed
guilty until proven innocent.

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:17.196
This drives the food industry crazy,

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:21.680
If additives are the problem, well then,

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:26.640
why not just stick to so-called clean label foods,

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:29.120
made with simple, recognizable ingredients,

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:31.600
no matter how the food is processed.

00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:35.520
But that’s assuming additives
are the only other reason

00:10:35.520 --> 00:10:37.600
that ultra-processed foods may be unhealthy.

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:40.000
Harmful additives are just one of many ways

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:42.720
ultra-processed foods have been linked to disease.

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:46.240
At least the food additives
are listed on the label;

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:47.920
so, we could avoid them if we wanted to.

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:51.360
Unlisted are some of the sneakier ways

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:54.000
ultra-processed foods may be unhealthy.

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:56.480
Harmful additives are just one of the ways

00:10:56.480 --> 00:11:00.400
that ultra-processed foods
have been linked to disease.

00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:03.520
Like advanced glycation end
products (AGEs), for example.

00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:06.960
Increasing evidence has shown
that uptake of dietary AGEs,

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:08.320
advanced glycation end products,

00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:11.600
is closely related to the
occurrence of many chronic diseases:

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:13.680
diabetes, chronic kidney disease,

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:15.440
osteoporosis, and Alzheimer’s disease.

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:19.680
In fact, it’s hard to find
a single age-related disease

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:23.280
not associated with AGEs.

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:26.560
Dietary AGEs are abundant
in highly processed food,

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:28.560
because thermal treatments are commonly used,

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:31.120
but thermal treatment just means heat;

00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:33.680
so, we can get AGEs from industrial processing

00:11:33.680 --> 00:11:35.806
or just from home cooking.

00:11:35.806 --> 00:11:41.520
So, let’s look at some examples.
Canned corn has 20 units of AGEs per serving,

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:46.800
compared to corn chips at 151
and corn pops cereal at 373.

00:11:47.920 --> 00:11:52.560
Rice only has 9, but rice crackers 275,

00:11:52.560 --> 00:11:56.080
and Rice Krispies has 600.

00:11:56.080 --> 00:11:58.560
So, you might be at the store

00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:01.760
and see some a no-salt-added rice crackers

00:12:01.760 --> 00:12:05.280
or a no-sugar-added puffed
rice cereal and be like,

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:10.480
“Oh, it’s just straight rice.
Perfect—one ingredient.”

00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:13.120
But no, because of the processing,

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:16.440
it’s not just that one ingredient.

00:12:16.440 --> 00:12:19.520
A boiled potato has 17 per serving,

00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:24.240
potato chips 865, and fast-food fries over 1,500.

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:29.120
So, ultra-processed plant foods can have
nearly 100 times more of these toxins

00:12:29.120 --> 00:12:32.000
than minimally-processed plant foods.

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:36.560
But even raw, unprocessed animal foods

00:12:36.560 --> 00:12:40.160
start out with high levels
and just go up from there.

00:12:40.160 --> 00:12:44.800
Instead of the 10 or 20 in
cooked whole plant foods,

00:12:44.800 --> 00:12:51.200
fish, poultry, and meat start out at
around 500 or more in their raw state

00:12:51.200 --> 00:12:55.760
and then jump into the thousands once cooked,

00:12:55.760 --> 00:12:59.120
which is still considered minimal processing,

00:12:59.120 --> 00:13:02.080
though processed or ultra-processed meat

00:13:02.080 --> 00:13:06.800
can exceed 10,000 per serving.

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:10.240
Although these contaminants are not
limited to ultra-processed foods,

00:13:10.240 --> 00:13:15.760
you can see how just considering foods
based on their listed ingredients

00:13:15.760 --> 00:13:19.840
fails to capture how food processing
can sometimes transform food

00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:22.240
at a molecular level.

00:13:22.240 --> 00:13:26.160
So, it’s clear that just using
this kind of clean label strategy

00:13:26.160 --> 00:13:29.840
of sticking to foods with simple
ingredient lists isn’t going to cut it,

00:13:29.840 --> 00:13:33.920
since you’re never going to read
something like AGEs on a label.

00:13:33.920 --> 00:13:37.440
It’s the processing itself,
whether traditional or industrial,

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:40.560
that is affecting the healthfulness of the food.

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:44.000
In the latest dietary guidance document
from the American Heart Association,

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:46.560
they advise choosing minimally processed foods,

00:13:46.560 --> 00:13:48.160
instead of ultra-processed foods.

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:50.400
Now, of course, they’re not
telling people to eat a steak,

00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:52.880
which is considered minimally processed,

00:13:52.880 --> 00:13:53.960
because they also emphasize

00:13:53.960 --> 00:13:57.520
that we should eat foods low in
saturated fat and cholesterol.

00:13:57.520 --> 00:14:01.920
But just like there can be unhealthy
unprocessed foods, like steak,

00:14:01.920 --> 00:14:07.840
they suggest there may be some
healthy ultra-processed foods,

00:14:07.840 --> 00:14:13.840
like what about, plant-based
milk and meat substitutes.

00:14:13.840 --> 00:14:16.320
Admonitions against the consumption of products

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:18.640
simply because they are classified
as ultra-processed foods

00:14:18.640 --> 00:14:23.040
may impair society’s acceptance
of a plant-based diet—

00:14:23.040 --> 00:14:26.960
thus preventing any related health
advantages from being realized.

00:14:26.960 --> 00:14:33.280
And indeed, that does appear
to be a significant reason

00:14:33.280 --> 00:14:37.920
why many people avoid these products.

00:14:37.920 --> 00:14:39.440
Is that a good thing?

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:40.720
Is that a bad thing?

00:14:40.720 --> 00:14:46.080
Are plant-based alternatives healthier
than conventional animal products?

00:14:46.080 --> 00:14:49.040
Let’s find out.

00:14:49.760 --> 00:14:52.640
Food Compass is a nutrient-profiling system

00:14:52.640 --> 00:14:56.240
that rates foods on a scale from 1 to 100,

00:14:56.240 --> 00:15:00.000
with 1 being least healthy
and 100 being most healthy.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:02.400
Eight thousand foods have been scored;

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:04.960
so, you can see where your favorite food falls.

00:15:04.960 --> 00:15:06.640
And when I have my laptop next to me,

00:15:06.640 --> 00:15:08.400
I ask people to yell out their favorite foods,

00:15:08.400 --> 00:15:10.320
and we look up to see what the score is,

00:15:10.320 --> 00:15:15.840
and they’re all disappointed that their
raspberries are only 93 or something,

00:15:17.760 --> 00:15:19.866
but unfortunately, laptop’s in the back.

00:15:20.800 --> 00:15:25.280
OK, so the main objective of
ultra-processed “fake” foods

00:15:25.280 --> 00:15:26.880
is to imitate real foods.

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:30.880
Like strawberry Pop Tarts trying
to mimic real strawberries.

00:15:31.520 --> 00:15:35.840
When you start with a perfect-100
food like strawberries,

00:15:35.840 --> 00:15:38.480
the only way to go is down.

00:15:38.480 --> 00:15:43.440
So, fruit is better than
fruity-shaped marshmallows.

00:15:43.440 --> 00:15:47.840
But when the “real” food is ultra-processed meat,

00:15:47.840 --> 00:15:51.920
processed meat, or unprocessed meat, or dairy,

00:15:51.920 --> 00:15:55.840
there is a lot of room for improvement.

00:15:55.840 --> 00:16:00.400
Food Compass has scored nine
ultra-processed meatless alternatives.

00:16:00.400 --> 00:16:04.160
Let’s compare them to the closest
conventional meat matches.

00:16:04.160 --> 00:16:07.520
In every single case, the
plant-based meat was rated higher,

00:16:07.520 --> 00:16:09.760
healthier, and sometimes by a large margin.

00:16:09.760 --> 00:16:12.320
Nearly all scored at least twice as high.

00:16:12.320 --> 00:16:17.280
Or three times the health score, seven times,

00:16:17.280 --> 00:16:20.480
20 times, even 60-fold higher.

00:16:20.480 --> 00:16:25.200
Other common nutrient scoring systems
all found the same general trend.

00:16:25.200 --> 00:16:29.600
Now, better than chicken would be chickpeas.

00:16:29.600 --> 00:16:33.120
And better soybeans than soy burgers—

00:16:33.120 --> 00:16:36.077
soybeans are another perfect-100 food,

00:16:36.240 --> 00:16:39.200
This was just a few products
though, just as an illustration.

00:16:39.200 --> 00:16:42.480
A 2024 systematic review identified nine studies

00:16:42.480 --> 00:16:48.080
using nutritional scoring for the
assessment of over 2,000 meat alternatives

00:16:48.080 --> 00:16:49.440
and their meaty matches,

00:16:49.440 --> 00:16:51.680
and all of the studies, nine out of nine,

00:16:51.680 --> 00:16:53.200
sometimes found comparable scores,

00:16:53.200 --> 00:16:56.800
but mostly showed meat alternatives score better.

00:16:56.800 --> 00:16:58.400
Based on their nutrient profiles,

00:16:58.400 --> 00:17:03.280
plant-based meats and milks would be
expected to reduce chronic disease risk.

00:17:03.280 --> 00:17:07.200
When it comes to heart
disease, stroke, and cancer,

00:17:07.200 --> 00:17:10.240
plant-based meats would be
expected to decrease risk

00:17:10.240 --> 00:17:13.280
of these killer diseases by about 3%

00:17:13.280 --> 00:17:16.160
per daily serving replacement.

00:17:16.160 --> 00:17:20.960
Now, whole soy foods might
take that down an extra 2%,

00:17:20.960 --> 00:17:23.760
because they’re not weighed
down by the sodium issue,

00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:26.880
but overall, swapping in even just one serving

00:17:26.880 --> 00:17:28.640
of plant-based meat a day nationwide

00:17:28.640 --> 00:17:33.360
could potentially prevent more than
100,000 cases of heart disease,

00:17:33.360 --> 00:17:36.800
stroke, or cancer in the U.S. every year.

00:17:37.520 --> 00:17:41.120
It’s hard to get everyone to go
all kale and quinoa overnight,

00:17:41.120 --> 00:17:43.520
but a veggie burger should be easy.

00:17:44.560 --> 00:17:47.593
100,000 cases prevented every year,

00:17:47.593 --> 00:17:52.080
with a single daily serving
swap of plant-based meat.

00:17:52.080 --> 00:17:55.080
Now, that’s just based on their
respective nutrient make-up—

00:17:55.080 --> 00:17:57.920
like how much saturated fat
versus fiber they each had.

00:17:57.920 --> 00:18:01.120
But remember how all the harmful effects

00:18:01.120 --> 00:18:06.640
of ultra-processed foods are not necessarily
captured by their nutrient profile?

00:18:06.640 --> 00:18:10.320
There are 16 proposed mechanisms

00:18:10.320 --> 00:18:14.240
linking ultra-processed
foods to disease and death,

00:18:14.240 --> 00:18:18.000
and balance of nutrients is only one of those 16.

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:20.960
And so, using these 16 criteria,

00:18:20.960 --> 00:18:24.320
normally, when you compare
ultra-processed products

00:18:24.320 --> 00:18:26.080
to the foods they were designed to replace,

00:18:26.080 --> 00:18:29.440
for example, water vs. Kool-Aid,

00:18:29.440 --> 00:18:32.400
or fruit vs. fruit-flavored candy,

00:18:32.400 --> 00:18:34.720
the ultra-processed product tends to do worse

00:18:34.720 --> 00:18:39.520
and not just because of the
junkier nutrient profile.

00:18:39.520 --> 00:18:44.720
However, plant-based meats
appear to be the exception,

00:18:44.720 --> 00:18:51.520
better in most ways compared to the
foods they were designed to replace.

00:18:51.520 --> 00:18:53.520
We already covered nutrient profile,

00:18:53.520 --> 00:18:57.360
where plant-based meat tended to
score healthier than regular meat,

00:18:57.360 --> 00:18:58.640
based on every study,

00:18:58.640 --> 00:19:01.442
using every major nutrient profiling system.

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:04.400
In terms of additives,

00:19:04.400 --> 00:19:09.440
I’ve been long concerned that Red
No. 3 hadn’t been banned from food,

00:19:09.440 --> 00:19:12.960
given that it was banned over 30 years ago

00:19:12.960 --> 00:19:17.680
from anything going on our
skin, due to cancer risk.

00:19:17.680 --> 00:19:21.120
But it’s still ok to eat?

00:19:21.120 --> 00:19:24.400
Thankfully, California passed a Food Safety Act,

00:19:24.400 --> 00:19:27.600
set to ban Red Dye No. 3 by 2027.

00:19:27.600 --> 00:19:37.680
So, in 2027, Loma Linda Big Franks
will be illegal to sell in Loma Linda

00:19:37.680 --> 00:19:41.200
but would still be available to
potentially contribute to cancer risk

00:19:41.200 --> 00:19:43.760
throughout the rest of the country.

00:19:43.760 --> 00:19:47.840
But, the FDA finally decided to ban it from food,

00:19:48.400 --> 00:19:51.200
35 years after it removed it from cosmetics,

00:19:51.200 --> 00:19:53.120
starting in 2027 as well.

00:19:53.120 --> 00:20:00.400
So, for now, this veggie bacon is
considered too toxic to rub on your skin

00:20:00.400 --> 00:20:01.760
but okay to eat—

00:20:01.760 --> 00:20:06.000
at least, for the next two years.

00:20:06.000 --> 00:20:11.680
Why has the pork bacon industry
figured out how to use cherry powder

00:20:11.680 --> 00:20:16.800
as a coloring before the
plant-based bacon industry?

00:20:16.800 --> 00:20:21.520
The most harmful additive in
plant-based meat, is ironically,

00:20:21.520 --> 00:20:24.080
really the most traditional and that’s salt,

00:20:24.080 --> 00:20:28.080
contributing to the #1 dietary risk
factor for death on planet Earth,

00:20:28.080 --> 00:20:30.080
excess sodium consumption.

00:20:30.080 --> 00:20:33.920
It is one of the most common ingredients
in plant-based meat alternatives.

00:20:33.920 --> 00:20:36.400
But, also one of the most frequent ingredients

00:20:36.400 --> 00:20:38.240
used in conventional meat processing,

00:20:38.240 --> 00:20:40.240
and not just in processed meat.

00:20:40.240 --> 00:20:44.720
While the #1 source of sodium for
kids in this country is pizza,

00:20:44.720 --> 00:20:47.040
and in older individuals bread,

00:20:47.040 --> 00:20:49.600
one of the reasons the #1 source of sodium

00:20:49.600 --> 00:20:53.040
for those aged 19 through 50 in this country

00:20:53.040 --> 00:20:59.520
is chicken is because injecting
raw chicken with saltwater

00:20:59.520 --> 00:21:03.520
is a widespread practice in the poultry industry,

00:21:03.520 --> 00:21:05.680
something they’ve been doing since the 1970s,

00:21:05.680 --> 00:21:10.560
resulting in raw chicken breasts reaching
as much as 400mg of sodium or more,

00:21:10.560 --> 00:21:14.160
six times more than they’d naturally have.

00:21:14.160 --> 00:21:18.480
When it comes to salt, how does
meat and plant-based meat compare?

00:21:18.480 --> 00:21:21.720
A systematic review found that
the average levels of sodium

00:21:21.720 --> 00:21:23.680
were not significantly different overall

00:21:23.680 --> 00:21:25.600
between plant-based and conventional meat,

00:21:25.600 --> 00:21:27.360
which is not great,

00:21:27.360 --> 00:21:29.624
but there are some products are improving.

00:21:29.920 --> 00:21:34.880
and Impossible now has a
beef “lite,” with only 260.

00:21:34.880 --> 00:21:37.840
Ideally, we would be under 180,

00:21:37.840 --> 00:21:40.800
meeting the World Health
Organization’s recommendation

00:21:40.800 --> 00:21:44.560
for less than a 1:1 ratio of sodium to calories.

00:21:45.200 --> 00:21:49.840
All the foods we eat ideally should
have less sodium than calories.

00:21:50.400 --> 00:21:53.600
Plant-based meat tends to be better
when it comes to some additives,

00:21:53.600 --> 00:21:56.640
like carcinogenic nitrite preservatives,

00:21:56.640 --> 00:22:00.960
similar with salt and sugar,
and worse with emulsifiers,

00:22:00.960 --> 00:22:05.040
which might be expected to
hurt the gut microbiome,

00:22:05.040 --> 00:22:08.560
but vegetarians who eat plant-based meat

00:22:08.560 --> 00:22:12.000
actually end up with lower rates
of irritable bowel syndrome

00:22:12.000 --> 00:22:13.040
than those who don’t,

00:22:13.040 --> 00:22:15.520
which suggests that at least on that metric

00:22:15.520 --> 00:22:17.840
the emulsifiers don’t seem to be a problem.

00:22:17.840 --> 00:22:22.000
In fact, all three studies on
the impact of plant-based meat

00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:24.960
show microbiome benefits;

00:22:24.960 --> 00:22:26.880
for example, this randomized, controlled trial

00:22:26.880 --> 00:22:28.960
analyzing stool samples before and after

00:22:28.960 --> 00:22:33.360
even just swapping out about
five meals of meat a week

00:22:33.360 --> 00:22:35.040
for plant-based meat,

00:22:35.040 --> 00:22:38.720
confirming that even just the
occasional replacement of meat

00:22:38.720 --> 00:22:42.640
may promote positive changes
in the gut microbiome.

00:22:42.640 --> 00:22:45.600
In the second trial, the Mycomeat study,

00:22:45.600 --> 00:22:48.560
people were randomized to replace
a few servings of meat a day

00:22:48.560 --> 00:22:51.440
with the mycoprotein meat Quorn—

00:22:51.440 --> 00:22:54.320
made from the mushroom kingdom
rather than the plant kingdom.

00:22:54.320 --> 00:22:58.720
This swap not only increases the abundance
of beneficial microbes in the gut,

00:22:58.720 --> 00:23:02.080
it reduces fecal genotoxicity,

00:23:02.080 --> 00:23:07.520
which is the amount of DNA damage that
their feces can cause colon cells.

00:23:07.520 --> 00:23:10.480
Now, that may just have been because
they were cutting down on meat,

00:23:11.200 --> 00:23:14.080
but on the Quorn, there was
a boost in good bacteria,

00:23:14.080 --> 00:23:18.480
like lactobacillus, which may have benefits,

00:23:18.480 --> 00:23:24.880
suggesting that the fiber in mycoprotein
may have prebiotic potential.

00:23:24.880 --> 00:23:27.840
The two-week swap did not
seem to be long enough time

00:23:27.840 --> 00:23:30.640
to affect TMAO levels, though,

00:23:30.640 --> 00:23:33.920
but in the 8-week Stanford SWAP-MEAT study,

00:23:33.920 --> 00:23:36.320
in which two or more servings
a day of beef, pork, or chicken

00:23:36.320 --> 00:23:39.760
were swapped out with plant-based
beef, pork, or chicken,

00:23:39.760 --> 00:23:42.080
several cardiovascular risk factors improved,

00:23:42.080 --> 00:23:46.480
including TMAO, which stands
for trimethylamine n-oxide,

00:23:46.480 --> 00:23:52.880
considered like the smoking gun in
gut microbiome-disease interactions.

00:23:52.880 --> 00:23:58.080
When we eat meat, dairy, or eggs,

00:23:58.080 --> 00:24:02.880
the carnitine and choline get turned by
certain bad gut bugs into trimethylamine,

00:24:02.880 --> 00:24:05.280
which is oxidized by our liver into TMAO

00:24:05.280 --> 00:24:09.920
which can go on to damage various
organ systems throughout the body.

00:24:09.920 --> 00:24:14.080
So, the cardiovascular harm from eggs and meat

00:24:14.080 --> 00:24:17.040
is more than just cholesterol and saturated fat.

00:24:17.040 --> 00:24:20.800
TMAO is linked to some of our deadliest diseases—

00:24:20.800 --> 00:24:24.882
cardiovascular disease, diabetes,
high blood pressure, stroke, cancer.

00:24:25.200 --> 00:24:28.800
Plant-based meals can also be better
for the upper digestive tract.

00:24:28.800 --> 00:24:32.800
If you randomize people to eat
chicken, cheese, fish, and steak,

00:24:32.800 --> 00:24:37.120
versus tofu, soy steaks, seitan, and soy burgers,

00:24:37.120 --> 00:24:41.200
you see twice as much heartburn
after the animal protein meal

00:24:41.200 --> 00:24:43.520
than after the plant-protein meal.

00:24:43.520 --> 00:24:47.600
And stick a probe down their throats,
and after the animal products,

00:24:47.600 --> 00:24:52.240
you see three times as much acid
exposure refluxing up from their stomach

00:24:53.920 --> 00:24:55.200
into their esophagus.

00:24:55.200 --> 00:24:57.760
The bottom line is that,

00:24:57.760 --> 00:25:00.880
unlike almost any other ultra-processed product,

00:25:00.880 --> 00:25:03.440
plant-based meat appears to be the rare case

00:25:03.440 --> 00:25:07.200
in which ultra-processed products
are actually better, overall,

00:25:07.200 --> 00:25:10.240
than the foods they were designed to replace.

00:25:10.240 --> 00:25:14.080
Now, this probably says less how healthy they are

00:25:14.080 --> 00:25:16.880
and more about how really
unhealthy modern meat is.

00:25:16.880 --> 00:25:20.080
Take food safety, for example.

00:25:20.080 --> 00:25:22.320
In response to a Super Bowl ad

00:25:22.320 --> 00:25:25.920
placed by a meat-industry-funded lobbying group

00:25:25.920 --> 00:25:31.680
questioning plant-based meat for
its hard-to-spell ingredients,

00:25:31.680 --> 00:25:34.720
Impossible Foods responded with a parody,

00:25:34.720 --> 00:25:39.200
questioning an unintentional
additive in conventional meat.

00:25:40.560 --> 00:25:44.480
Fecal contamination of the
carcass in the slaughter plant

00:25:44.480 --> 00:25:47.120
is considered unavoidable,

00:25:47.120 --> 00:25:53.280
and though there are methods for removing
visible fecal contamination on meat,

00:25:53.280 --> 00:25:56.000
and even experimental imaging technologies

00:25:56.640 --> 00:26:00.240
designed to detect more
“diluted fecal contaminations,”

00:26:00.240 --> 00:26:05.600
we’re still left at a retail level
with guess what proportion of meat

00:26:05.600 --> 00:26:09.360
contaminated with fecal
bacteria at the grocery store?

00:26:09.360 --> 00:26:14.880
About 85% of ground beef and turkey
contaminated with fecal bacteria,

00:26:14.880 --> 00:26:19.280
and about half of chicken on store
shelves, and a third of pork,

00:26:19.280 --> 00:26:21.360
found contaminated with Enterococci,

00:26:21.360 --> 00:26:26.960
which is used as a marker of
fecal contamination of food.

00:26:26.960 --> 00:26:31.040
But you don’t have to cook the
crap out of plant-based meat,

00:26:31.040 --> 00:26:34.960
because there shouldn’t be any crap to begin with.

00:26:35.520 --> 00:26:40.160
Fecal contamination is not just unseemly
but a critical public health issue.

00:26:40.160 --> 00:26:43.120
Fecal matter is considered the main source

00:26:43.120 --> 00:26:45.040
of some of our most serious foodborne pathogens

00:26:45.040 --> 00:26:47.520
like Salmonella and Campylobacter.

00:26:47.520 --> 00:26:51.520
Salmonella is the leading cause of foodborne
hospitalizations in the United States

00:26:51.520 --> 00:26:54.240
and the #1 cause of foodborne death,

00:26:54.240 --> 00:26:59.600
and 1 in 4 packages of chicken sampled
in the United States by the USDA

00:26:59.600 --> 00:27:02.080
contaminated with Salmonella,

00:27:02.080 --> 00:27:05.440
1 in 8 packages of ground turkey,
1 in 24 packages of pork,

00:27:05.440 --> 00:27:07.680
and 1 in 67 packages of ground beef,

00:27:07.680 --> 00:27:10.960
and 1 in 4 packages of retail chicken.

00:27:10.960 --> 00:27:17.400
Campylobacter, which sickens more
than 800,000 Americans a year,

00:27:17.400 --> 00:27:22.160
is also found in every 1 in 3 or
1 in 4 packages of retail chicken.

00:27:22.160 --> 00:27:25.840
Even just shopping for meat can be risky,

00:27:25.840 --> 00:27:30.640
as disease-causing fecal bacteria can
contaminate the outer packaging of meat.

00:27:30.640 --> 00:27:36.560
But, the good news is that food-borne
illnesses are largely preventable,

00:27:36.560 --> 00:27:40.080
and the simplest approach
to reduce their occurrence

00:27:40.080 --> 00:27:44.240
is to greatly reduce the consumption
of animal products like meat.

00:27:44.240 --> 00:27:46.240
They’re intestinal bugs,

00:27:46.240 --> 00:27:48.800
and since plants don’t have any intestines,

00:27:48.800 --> 00:27:51.520
we can reduce the risk with plant-based meat,

00:27:52.480 --> 00:27:54.640
though there are always cases of
manure run-off contaminating crops.

00:27:55.760 --> 00:28:02.960
But the leading causes of large food-borne
outbreaks of food poisoning are all meat,

00:28:02.960 --> 00:28:06.960
and every single one of the
top pathogen-food combinations

00:28:06.960 --> 00:28:10.160
that cause the greatest burden of
disease are all animal products,

00:28:10.160 --> 00:28:11.840
mostly meat.

00:28:11.840 --> 00:28:14.000
That second one is toxoplasma,

00:28:14.000 --> 00:28:20.640
which is a brain parasite that may
infect a million Americans a year,

00:28:20.640 --> 00:28:26.240
making it a leading source of severe
foodborne illness in the United States.

00:28:26.240 --> 00:28:32.880
What may end up in your brain
may start in your burger.

00:28:32.880 --> 00:28:36.080
Tapeworms in your brain from pork

00:28:36.080 --> 00:28:39.680
have become an increasingly important
emerging infection in the United States.

00:28:39.680 --> 00:28:46.151
What you think is a migraine
may not be a migraine.

00:28:46.240 --> 00:28:50.240
You may even get bladder
infections from eating meat,

00:28:50.240 --> 00:28:53.120
particularly poultry, urinary tract infections,

00:28:53.120 --> 00:28:57.920
or viral hepatitis from pork
products—hepatitis E infection.

00:28:57.920 --> 00:29:00.800
There aren’t any prions in plants either,

00:29:00.800 --> 00:29:03.760
the cause of fatal spongiform brain diseases,

00:29:03.760 --> 00:29:06.800
which is why there’s a mad cow disease,

00:29:06.800 --> 00:29:10.560
but not a mad Quorn disease.

00:29:12.880 --> 00:29:16.080
And I didn’t include other meat-borne pathogens,

00:29:16.080 --> 00:29:18.800
like trichinosis or E. coli O157:H7,

00:29:18.800 --> 00:29:22.560
which still sickens 50,000
Americans a year, mostly from beef,

00:29:22.560 --> 00:29:27.280
or the 100,000 sickened by
yersinia-infected pork every year,

00:29:27.280 --> 00:29:29.600
but let’s just stick to the major pathogens,

00:29:29.600 --> 00:29:34.320
some of which can become antibiotic-resistant.

00:29:34.320 --> 00:29:37.760
Consuming contaminated meat with
antibiotic-resistant bacteria

00:29:37.760 --> 00:29:41.600
is considered a severe public health hazard.

00:29:41.600 --> 00:29:46.720
To help compensate for the overcrowded,
stressful, unhygienic conditions,

00:29:46.720 --> 00:29:50.720
animals raised for food are
dosed en masse with antibiotics.

00:29:50.720 --> 00:29:53.760
Lots of antibiotics. This is in kilos;

00:29:53.760 --> 00:30:01.520
so, we’re talking 10 million pounds a
year of medically-important antibiotics.

00:30:01.520 --> 00:30:04.000
So, we give farm animals in the United States

00:30:04.000 --> 00:30:07.440
more than a million pounds
of penicillin drugs a year.

00:30:07.440 --> 00:30:10.240
Four thousand tons of tetracyclines.

00:30:11.520 --> 00:30:16.400
Millions of pounds of drugs, laced
right into their food and water.

00:30:16.400 --> 00:30:18.880
Antibiotics important to human medicine

00:30:18.880 --> 00:30:21.600
being fed to cows, pigs, chickens by the ton—

00:30:21.600 --> 00:30:25.520
by the thousands of tons every year.

00:30:25.520 --> 00:30:28.160
And so now in chicken breasts, for example,

00:30:28.160 --> 00:30:31.360
most of the Salmonella and
Campylobacter found is now resistant

00:30:31.360 --> 00:30:33.080
to at least one class of antibiotics,

00:30:33.080 --> 00:30:36.160
and about half of the Salmonella is resistant

00:30:36.160 --> 00:30:39.040
to three or more classes of drugs.

00:30:39.040 --> 00:30:43.760
Even organic meat raised without antibiotics

00:30:43.760 --> 00:30:46.320
can be contaminated with
multidrug-resistant bacteria,

00:30:46.320 --> 00:30:48.960
often because there is cross-contamination
in the slaughter plant.

00:30:48.960 --> 00:30:52.400
It’s funny—in the cover story of Consumer Reports

00:30:52.400 --> 00:30:55.760
that urged retailers to stop selling
meat produced with antibiotics,

00:30:55.760 --> 00:30:59.520
they noted, some store employee confusion:

00:30:59.520 --> 00:31:01.120
“An assistant manager at one store,

00:31:01.120 --> 00:31:05.040
when asked by a shopper for
meats raised without antibiotics,

00:31:05.040 --> 00:31:12.560
responded, ‘Wait, you mean,
like, veggie burgers?'” Exactly.

00:31:12.560 --> 00:31:16.000
Antibiotic-resistance genes can
also be transmitted through meat,

00:31:16.000 --> 00:31:18.880
which can then transfer to
other pathogens in our gut,

00:31:18.880 --> 00:31:22.560
as well as residues of the
antibiotic drugs themselves,

00:31:22.560 --> 00:31:25.680
which is itself considered a
serious public health threat.

00:31:25.680 --> 00:31:28.640
You can correlate how much meat people eat;

00:31:28.640 --> 00:31:30.960
you can just measure the amount of antibiotics

00:31:30.960 --> 00:31:34.000
flowing out of their urine every day.

00:31:35.440 --> 00:31:38.720
These drug residues in the
meat can cause allergies,

00:31:38.720 --> 00:31:41.520
nerve damage, liver damage,
reproductive disorders,

00:31:41.520 --> 00:31:44.800
bone marrow toxicity, even increased cancer risk,

00:31:44.800 --> 00:31:50.880
not to mention what eating antibiotics
every day is doing to your gut microbiome.

00:31:50.880 --> 00:31:53.840
In contrast, the production of
plant-based meat involves no guts,

00:31:53.840 --> 00:31:57.440
no feces, no antibiotics necessary.

00:31:57.440 --> 00:32:01.680
In addition to antibiotics, livestock
may be fed or injected with hormones.

00:32:01.680 --> 00:32:04.240
Currently, seven hormone
drugs are approved by the FDA

00:32:04.240 --> 00:32:06.000
to bulk up the production of milk and meat.

00:32:06.000 --> 00:32:11.040
In Europe, there exists a
total ban on all such use.

00:32:11.040 --> 00:32:13.360
But even without injected hormones,

00:32:13.360 --> 00:32:15.360
animal products naturally contain hormones

00:32:15.360 --> 00:32:18.560
because they come from animals.

00:32:18.560 --> 00:32:21.600
I already touched on processing contaminants,

00:32:21.600 --> 00:32:24.960
but there are also industrial contaminants
that build up in the food chain,

00:32:24.960 --> 00:32:27.680
like certain pesticides, PCBs, heavy metals,

00:32:27.680 --> 00:32:31.120
and ﬂame retardant chemicals that
end up concentrating in meat.

00:32:31.120 --> 00:32:34.480
Can’t you just choose organic meat?

00:32:34.480 --> 00:32:37.760
Surprisingly, the consumption of organic meat

00:32:37.760 --> 00:32:41.040
does not seem to diminish
the carcinogenic potential

00:32:41.040 --> 00:32:44.640
associated with these industrial pollutants.

00:32:44.640 --> 00:32:47.600
If you look at the micropollutants
and chemical residues

00:32:47.600 --> 00:32:51.360
in conventional vs. organic meat,

00:32:51.360 --> 00:32:55.760
several environmental contaminants—like
dioxins, PCBs, lead, arsenic

00:32:55.760 --> 00:33:00.880
sometimes measured at signiﬁcantly
higher levels in organic meat,

00:33:00.880 --> 00:33:03.760
though cooking does draw off some of the fat

00:33:03.760 --> 00:33:06.560
where the PCBs are concentrated.

00:33:06.560 --> 00:33:08.800
Seafood seems to be the exception, though,

00:33:08.800 --> 00:33:10.160
with steaming, for example,

00:33:10.160 --> 00:33:14.960
generally increasing the concentration
of contaminants like mercury.

00:33:14.960 --> 00:33:21.680
There is, however, no mercury
added to plant-based tuna.

00:33:21.680 --> 00:33:24.320
When researchers tested retail meat

00:33:24.320 --> 00:33:28.640
for the presence of 33 chemicals with
calculated carcinogenic potential,

00:33:28.640 --> 00:33:35.360
like organochlorine pesticides
like DDT and dioxin-like PCBs,

00:33:35.360 --> 00:33:38.720
they concluded in order to
reduce our risk of cancer,

00:33:38.720 --> 00:33:41.840
we should limit our ingestion
of beef, pork, or chicken

00:33:41.840 --> 00:33:47.040
to a maximum of five servings—a month.

00:33:47.040 --> 00:33:50.400
Meat presents the only scenario

00:33:50.400 --> 00:33:53.680
in which ultra-processed products
were designed to replace foods

00:33:53.680 --> 00:33:55.920
that we know cause cancer,

00:33:55.920 --> 00:34:00.560
in fact, the #1 cancer killer of
nonsmokers, colorectal cancer.

00:34:01.280 --> 00:34:06.240
These are foods in desperate need of replacement.

00:34:06.240 --> 00:34:10.000
Processed meat—bacon, ham,
hot dogs, lunch meat, sausage—

00:34:10.000 --> 00:34:13.760
is considered a known human carcinogen,

00:34:13.760 --> 00:34:17.440
leading major cancer centers, like Mount Sinai,

00:34:17.440 --> 00:34:20.400
Memorial Sloan Kettering, and MD Anderson,

00:34:20.400 --> 00:34:24.640
to recommend flat-out avoid processed meat.

00:34:24.640 --> 00:34:28.240
All in all, plant-based meat is
better than animal-based meat

00:34:28.240 --> 00:34:29.840
in about 40 different ways.

00:34:29.840 --> 00:34:34.160
Now obviously, some of these categories
are more important than others.

00:34:34.160 --> 00:34:38.480
The fact that bacon is officially
designated as cancer-causing

00:34:38.480 --> 00:34:42.160
is more important than the fact
that it may have a few more calories

00:34:42.160 --> 00:34:44.000
than plant-based bacon.

00:34:44.000 --> 00:34:46.400
But cancer is just killer #2.

00:34:47.440 --> 00:34:51.600
The leading cause of death in
men and women is heart disease,

00:34:51.600 --> 00:34:53.520
and hidden in that nutrient profile,

00:34:53.520 --> 00:34:55.920
are the three things that raise LDL cholesterol:

00:34:55.920 --> 00:34:58.880
saturated fat, trans fat, and dietary cholesterol.

00:34:58.880 --> 00:35:02.560
Yes, there are other factors,
like TMAO and sodium.

00:35:02.560 --> 00:35:05.160
But LDL cholesterol, also
known as bad cholesterol,

00:35:05.160 --> 00:35:09.261
is unequivocally recognized
as the principal driver

00:35:09.261 --> 00:35:11.760
in the development of atherosclerotic
cardiovascular disease,

00:35:11.760 --> 00:35:15.200
our leading cause of death.

00:35:15.200 --> 00:35:22.960
Basically, we should try to
get our LDL as low as possible.

00:35:22.960 --> 00:35:27.040
When it comes to LDL lowering for
the prevention of heart disease,

00:35:27.040 --> 00:35:29.600
“Lower for longer is better.”

00:35:29.600 --> 00:35:33.520
Even if your LDL is quote-unquote normal;

00:35:33.520 --> 00:35:37.520
even if other heart disease risk
factors are considered optimal,

00:35:37.520 --> 00:35:41.920
still, of utmost importance to control it.

00:35:41.920 --> 00:35:46.720
So, if LDL is the primary
driver of our primary killer,

00:35:46.720 --> 00:35:48.960
if you could just know one thing about a food,

00:35:48.960 --> 00:35:51.040
if could just ask one question,

00:35:51.040 --> 00:35:55.760
it would be “What does this
food do to my LDL cholesterol?”

00:35:55.760 --> 00:35:59.680
So, we’re looking at nutrition labels for
saturated fat, trans fat, and cholesterol,

00:35:59.680 --> 00:36:04.320
since any intake above zero increases
LDL cholesterol and, therefore,

00:36:04.320 --> 00:36:07.200
increases our risk of coronary heart disease.

00:36:07.200 --> 00:36:09.120
In terms of saturated fat and cholesterol,

00:36:09.120 --> 00:36:12.240
animal meats are the main
source in the American diet,

00:36:12.240 --> 00:36:17.280
and with the removal of partially
hydrogenated oils from the U.S. food supply,

00:36:17.840 --> 00:36:22.000
such that these other sources are now banned,

00:36:22.000 --> 00:36:25.040
animal products are now the
leading source of trans fat as well

00:36:25.040 --> 00:36:29.200
(since meat and dairy
naturally contain trans fat).

00:36:29.200 --> 00:36:34.240
Online, some bloggers parrot
egg industry propaganda

00:36:34.240 --> 00:36:39.680
that the 2015 U.S. Dietary Guidelines
removed its dietary cholesterol limit,

00:36:39.680 --> 00:36:44.160
whereas if anyone actually
bothered to read the guidelines,

00:36:44.160 --> 00:36:46.960
they’d see that the guidelines actually
strengthened their recommendation,

00:36:46.960 --> 00:36:52.800
telling Americans to “eat as little
dietary cholesterol as possible,”

00:36:52.800 --> 00:36:54.840
as recommended by the Institute of Medicine,

00:36:54.840 --> 00:36:58.320
the most prestigious medical
body in the United States.

00:36:58.320 --> 00:37:01.800
This advice was reiterated in the
subsequent dietary guidelines:

00:37:01.800 --> 00:37:03.920
“The National Academies recommends

00:37:03.920 --> 00:37:09.200
that dietary cholesterol consumption
to be as low as possible.”

00:37:09.200 --> 00:37:12.800
While eggs are the most concentrated
source of cholesterol gram-for-gram,

00:37:12.800 --> 00:37:16.880
the greatest cholesterol contribution
in the overall American diet is meat,

00:37:16.880 --> 00:37:18.800
including poultry and fish.

00:37:18.800 --> 00:37:21.760
Since cholesterol is present
only in animal-derived foods,

00:37:21.760 --> 00:37:24.240
it’s not surprising that plant-based
meat contains little or no cholesterol

00:37:24.240 --> 00:37:26.960
(unless they have cheese
or something added in it).

00:37:26.960 --> 00:37:32.240
And saturated fat content is
usually low in plant-based meat

00:37:32.240 --> 00:37:35.600
compared to regular meat, averaging
to one half and one third.

00:37:35.760 --> 00:37:39.760
This is as true in Europe as well
as in South Africa and the U.S.—

00:37:39.760 --> 00:37:45.280
where plant-based meat may only
average a third of the saturated fat.

00:37:45.280 --> 00:37:48.800
Swapping out meat for plant-based meat
significantly lowers saturated fat,

00:37:48.800 --> 00:37:51.920
but does this translate into lower LDL?

00:37:51.920 --> 00:37:53.440
That’s really what we care about.

00:37:53.440 --> 00:38:00.560
Well, you don’t really know,
until you put it to the test.

00:38:00.560 --> 00:38:05.280
Here’s a meta-analysis of 10
randomized controlled trials

00:38:05.280 --> 00:38:08.640
replacing some or all meat with
plant-based or mycoprotein-based meats,

00:38:08.640 --> 00:38:10.560
and within an average of six weeks,

00:38:10.560 --> 00:38:13.280
saw a 15-point drop in LDL cholesterol.

00:38:13.280 --> 00:38:15.600
What does that mean in terms of disease risk?

00:38:15.600 --> 00:38:19.680
Maintaining a 15-point drop in LDL for five years

00:38:19.680 --> 00:38:22.920
would be expected to decrease your
risk of heart disease by about 10%,

00:38:22.920 --> 00:38:25.840
and after a dozen years by more like 15%,

00:38:25.840 --> 00:38:29.600
and across a lifetime by more like 25%.

00:38:30.640 --> 00:38:34.560
And risk reduction is independent of baseline LDL,

00:38:34.560 --> 00:38:37.920
meaning you get about the
same relative risk reduction,

00:38:37.920 --> 00:38:42.000
even if your LDL is so-called
normal, less than 100.

00:38:43.440 --> 00:38:48.160
One would also expect the use of egg
substitutes to reduce cardiovascular risk,

00:38:48.160 --> 00:38:51.120
given that eggs are the most concentrated
source of dietary cholesterol.

00:38:51.120 --> 00:38:56.080
What about substituting soymilk for cow’s milk?

00:38:56.080 --> 00:38:58.720
Based on 17 randomized, controlled trials,

00:38:58.720 --> 00:39:00.400
drinking soymilk instead of cow’s milk

00:39:00.400 --> 00:39:03.920
resulted in significant
improvements in blood pressure,

00:39:03.920 --> 00:39:06.880
in cholesterol, in inflammation,

00:39:06.880 --> 00:39:09.765
an 8-point drop in systolic blood pressure,

00:39:09.765 --> 00:39:12.515
5-point drop in diastolic blood pressure,

00:39:12.558 --> 00:39:15.600
a 7-point drop in LDL
cholesterol switching to soy,

00:39:15.600 --> 00:39:17.360
as well as a drop in c‑reactive protein,

00:39:17.360 --> 00:39:19.200
so less inflammation as well.

00:39:19.200 --> 00:39:20.640
Over a lifetime,

00:39:20.640 --> 00:39:25.280
that 7-point drop in LDL could drop our
risk of heart disease by more than 10%,

00:39:25.280 --> 00:39:30.000
just making a single dietary swap from cow to soy.

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:33.440
Now, the benefit only accrues
if your cholesterol stays down.

00:39:33.440 --> 00:39:36.080
And it turns out, it may even get better.

00:39:36.080 --> 00:39:38.800
In a four-year study in which
diabetics were randomized

00:39:38.800 --> 00:39:42.960
to swap out half their animal protein
for ultra-processed plant protein,

00:39:42.960 --> 00:39:46.000
their LDL went down year after year,

00:39:46.000 --> 00:39:49.840
ending up 26 points lower than the control group.

00:39:49.840 --> 00:39:54.560
Whoa, that could net you a nearly
40% drop in risk, over a lifetime,

00:39:54.560 --> 00:40:00.080
of our #1 killer, just swapping out
half of their animal protein servings.

00:40:00.080 --> 00:40:05.120
An estimated 20 million
Americans have heart disease,

00:40:06.000 --> 00:40:09.600
having about 800,000 heart attacks a year.

00:40:09.600 --> 00:40:13.440
If we started swapping our animal
products for plant products,

00:40:13.440 --> 00:40:18.480
imagine how many lives could be saved
by even a 10% drop in heart disease.

00:40:18.480 --> 00:40:20.800
Instead of swapping out a
burger for a veggie burger,

00:40:20.800 --> 00:40:23.440
why not just switch to chicken and fish?

00:40:23.440 --> 00:40:26.560
Because, it doesn’t work.

00:40:26.560 --> 00:40:28.640
A meta-analysis of randomized, controlled trials

00:40:28.640 --> 00:40:34.400
that compared the cholesterol effects
of beef versus poultry-and/or-ﬁsh

00:40:34.400 --> 00:40:37.680
found no significant difference in terms of LDL.

00:40:37.680 --> 00:40:40.640
And an updated meta-analysis
of four times as many studies

00:40:40.640 --> 00:40:44.640
also found no benefit to
switching to chicken and poultry,

00:40:44.640 --> 00:40:48.480
and the beef was actually
found to be better than fish,

00:40:48.480 --> 00:40:52.560
which they attribute to using particularly
lean cuts of beef as the comparator,

00:40:52.560 --> 00:40:55.120
particularly because the beef industry
is the one that funded these studies.

00:40:56.400 --> 00:41:02.480
But even organic, grass-fed beef appeared
to be no match for plant-based meat,

00:41:02.480 --> 00:41:07.840
as shown in the Stanford SWAP-MEAT trial.

00:41:07.840 --> 00:41:11.280
With processed meat officially
classified as a known human carcinogen,

00:41:11.280 --> 00:41:16.080
global health organizations flat-out
recommend avoid processed meat,

00:41:16.080 --> 00:41:19.280
but looking at six health outcomes—

00:41:19.280 --> 00:41:21.760
colorectal cancer, breast cancer,

00:41:21.760 --> 00:41:23.440
ischemic heart disease, type 2 diabetes,

00:41:23.440 --> 00:41:25.040
and two types of stroke—

00:41:25.040 --> 00:41:30.640
the optimal amount of unprocessed
red meat may be zero as well,

00:41:30.640 --> 00:41:33.440
but even a 30% reduction in both types of meat

00:41:33.440 --> 00:41:36.320
could lead to a million fewer
cases of type 2 diabetes,

00:41:36.320 --> 00:41:38.640
hundreds of thousands of fewer
cases of cardiovascular disease,

00:41:38.640 --> 00:41:43.200
and tens of thousands fewer cases of
cancer and premature death over a decade.

00:41:43.200 --> 00:41:46.080
Diets high in processed and red meat

00:41:46.080 --> 00:41:50.320
are also the two single leading causes of
diet-related disability in the United States,

00:41:50.320 --> 00:41:54.720
responsible for more than a million
years lived in disability annually—

00:41:54.720 --> 00:42:00.160
three times more than diets high in
sugar-sweetened beverages, for example,

00:42:00.160 --> 00:42:04.240
and more than two million
years of healthy life lost,

00:42:04.240 --> 00:42:06.870
based on the most comprehensive
analysis of risk factors

00:42:06.870 --> 00:42:08.800
for death and disability in history.

00:42:08.800 --> 00:42:11.280
Millions of years of lost
life every year in the U.S.

00:42:11.280 --> 00:42:14.880
because people are eating diets
high in red and processed meat.

00:42:15.600 --> 00:42:18.800
What do they define as high, though?

00:42:18.800 --> 00:42:27.680
A diet “high” in processed meat is
defined as any intake of processed meat.

00:42:27.680 --> 00:42:30.560
And a diet high in red meat?

00:42:30.560 --> 00:42:35.840
High as in any intake of red meat.

00:42:35.840 --> 00:42:39.120
So, millions of years of life
lost in the United States,

00:42:39.120 --> 00:42:43.440
because people are eating more
than zero red and processed meat.

00:42:43.440 --> 00:42:44.960
Higher intake of meat in general—

00:42:44.960 --> 00:42:48.320
red meat, white meat, processed, unprocessed—

00:42:48.320 --> 00:42:53.200
also associated with an increased risk
of death from all-causes put together.

00:42:53.200 --> 00:42:55.760
If people swapped out 75% of their meat,

00:42:55.760 --> 00:43:00.240
up to 50,000 lives a year could
be saved in high income countries.

00:43:00.240 --> 00:43:04.800
What would happen if the
whole world cut out all meat?

00:43:05.920 --> 00:43:11.920
We would save an estimated 7 million lives a year,

00:43:11.920 --> 00:43:16.240
and if humanity cut out all animal products,

00:43:16.240 --> 00:43:20.720
we might annually save 8 million lives.

00:43:20.720 --> 00:43:23.040
Hanging in the balance!

00:43:23.040 --> 00:43:28.400
The bottom line is that veggie
burgers are not Twinkies,

00:43:28.400 --> 00:43:30.960
even though they’re both classified
as ultra-processed foods.

00:43:30.960 --> 00:43:35.040
Now, there are two directions
we can go with that fact.

00:43:35.040 --> 00:43:39.200
We can decry the very concept
of ultra-processed foods.

00:43:39.200 --> 00:43:42.400
Or we can take what I would
argue is a better tact:

00:43:43.040 --> 00:43:45.120
keep the baby; throw out the bathwater.

00:43:45.120 --> 00:43:49.520
In other words, not deny
ultra-processed foods tend to be worse,

00:43:49.520 --> 00:43:51.920
not deny that plant-based
meats are ultra-processed,

00:43:51.920 --> 00:43:54.640
but rather that despite this classification,

00:43:54.640 --> 00:43:57.000
plant-based meats and milks are the rare exception

00:43:57.000 --> 00:44:00.080
in that they compare well with the
foods they were designed to replace,

00:44:00.800 --> 00:44:04.560
in some cases, potentially
even saving thousands of lives.

00:44:04.560 --> 00:44:06.800
But there is some merit to the argument

00:44:06.800 --> 00:44:11.760
that the ultra-processed concept is
not as useful as many people think.

00:44:11.760 --> 00:44:14.880
If you look at the three big Harvard cohorts—

00:44:14.880 --> 00:44:16.720
200,000 people followed for decades.

00:44:16.720 --> 00:44:20.880
Yes, total ultra-processed foods intake
was associated with cardiovascular risk,

00:44:20.880 --> 00:44:22.720
but if you break it down,

00:44:22.720 --> 00:44:28.560
the risk is driven exclusively by soda and meat.

00:44:28.560 --> 00:44:29.600
Sugar-sweetened beverages,

00:44:29.600 --> 00:44:31.440
a little from artificially sweetened beverages,

00:44:31.440 --> 00:44:34.880
and the rest from processed meat, poultry, fish.

00:44:34.880 --> 00:44:37.440
If you exclude soda and meat,

00:44:37.440 --> 00:44:41.680
the relationship between ultra-processed
food and cardiovascular disease

00:44:41.680 --> 00:44:44.000
disappears completely.

00:44:44.000 --> 00:44:46.880
So, nutritional advice for cardiovascular health

00:44:46.880 --> 00:44:48.800
should consider the differential consequences

00:44:48.800 --> 00:44:51.840
of group-speciﬁc ultra-processed foods,

00:44:51.840 --> 00:44:56.160
and speciﬁcally, that means
processed meats and soft drinks

00:44:56.160 --> 00:44:57.920
should be discouraged.

00:44:57.920 --> 00:45:00.080
What about mortality?

00:45:00.080 --> 00:45:02.794
Yes, those who ate the most
ultra-processed foods in general

00:45:02.794 --> 00:45:04.720
had a higher risk of dying prematurely,

00:45:04.720 --> 00:45:07.120
but it mattered what kind
of ultra-processed foods,

00:45:07.120 --> 00:45:10.480
with ready-to-eat meat,
poultry, and seafood products

00:45:10.480 --> 00:45:13.600
showing particularly strong
associations with mortality.

00:45:13.600 --> 00:45:16.080
The apparent worst ultra-processed food

00:45:16.080 --> 00:45:19.120
when it comes to dying prematurely in general?

00:45:19.120 --> 00:45:21.040
Meat/poultry/seafood.

00:45:21.040 --> 00:45:24.560
The worst when it comes to dying from cancer?

00:45:24.560 --> 00:45:25.840
Meat/poultry/seafood.

00:45:25.840 --> 00:45:29.440
The worst when it comes to dying
from cardiovascular disease?

00:45:29.440 --> 00:45:30.480
Meat/poultry/seafood.

00:45:30.480 --> 00:45:34.400
The worst when it comes to dying
from lung diseases like emphysema?

00:45:34.400 --> 00:45:35.440
Meat/poultry/seafood.

00:45:35.440 --> 00:45:39.360
The worst when it comes to dying
from neurodegenerative diseases?

00:45:39.360 --> 00:45:41.120
(Ice cream).

00:45:41.120 --> 00:45:45.520
And the worst when it comes
to dying from other causes?

00:45:45.520 --> 00:45:48.160
Meat/poultry/seafood.

00:45:48.160 --> 00:45:50.160
Bottom line,

00:45:50.160 --> 00:45:52.800
the major factors contributing
to the harmful effects

00:45:52.800 --> 00:45:58.720
of ultra-processed foods on mortality are
processed meat, poultry, fish, and soda.

00:45:58.720 --> 00:46:02.640
So, the negative, life-shortening
effects of ultra-processed food

00:46:02.640 --> 00:46:05.200
is really mostly talking about the
negative, life-shortening effect

00:46:05.200 --> 00:46:09.280
of ultra-processed meat like burgers,
chicken nuggets, and fish sticks.

00:46:10.240 --> 00:46:13.600
Ideally, we would have a study that
specifically looked at meat alternatives.

00:46:13.600 --> 00:46:17.040
And, here we go: a quarter-million
people followed for over a decade,

00:46:17.040 --> 00:46:19.680
and while the higher consumption
of ultra-processed foods in general

00:46:19.680 --> 00:46:23.200
increases the risk of cancer and
cardiovascular disease and diabetes,

00:46:23.200 --> 00:46:28.640
but that was driven by ultra-processed
animal products and soda,

00:46:28.640 --> 00:46:31.200
not plant-based meats.

00:46:31.200 --> 00:46:35.440
They specifically studied plant-based
alternatives and found no increased risk.

00:46:35.440 --> 00:46:38.000
And when it comes to diabetes,

00:46:38.000 --> 00:46:43.680
plant-based meat and milk appears to cut
the risk of developing diabetes in half.

00:46:44.320 --> 00:46:49.120
Animal-based products were associated
with more than twice the risk;

00:46:49.120 --> 00:46:53.440
plant-based alternatives with less than half.

00:46:53.440 --> 00:46:55.840
So, when the authors conclude
that higher consumption

00:46:55.840 --> 00:46:58.880
of ultra-processed foods may increase
the risk of cancer and other diseases,

00:46:58.880 --> 00:47:01.680
their data really only show

00:47:01.680 --> 00:47:05.920
that the consumption of foods
of animal origin and soft drinks

00:47:05.920 --> 00:47:07.680
is associated with such a risk,

00:47:07.680 --> 00:47:10.640
and indeed, when the authors were challenged

00:47:10.640 --> 00:47:14.160
to go back and exclude animal foods and beverages,

00:47:14.160 --> 00:47:20.240
the relationship between ultra-processed
foods and multiple diseases disappears.

00:47:20.240 --> 00:47:24.640
That’s also what a systematic review and
meta-analysis of all such studies showed.

00:47:24.640 --> 00:47:29.280
The only categories of ultra-processed
foods associated with higher mortality

00:47:29.280 --> 00:47:33.600
were sweetened beverages and meat.

00:47:33.600 --> 00:47:39.520
So wait, iIf the only ultra-processed food that
appears to be killing people is meat,

00:47:39.520 --> 00:47:42.202
then in that case, instead of
being a part of the problem,

00:47:42.202 --> 00:47:48.638
plant-based meats may be the solution to the ultra-processed problem. 
Right, let me say that again. 

00:47:48.638 --> 00:47:54.960
Instead of being part of the problem, plant-based meats 
may be the solution to the ultra-processed problem.

00:47:54.960 --> 00:47:58.560
The one other ultra-processed study
that separated out plant-based meats

00:47:58.560 --> 00:48:02.480
looked at telomere length, which
is used to measure cellular aging.

00:48:02.480 --> 00:48:06.080
Their study found that a higher
consumption of total ultra-processed foods

00:48:06.080 --> 00:48:07.920
was associated with a shorter telomere length,

00:48:07.920 --> 00:48:09.520
a sign of accelerated aging.

00:48:09.520 --> 00:48:12.240
However, some subclasses of ultra-processed foods

00:48:12.240 --> 00:48:14.640
were associated with longer telomere length,

00:48:14.640 --> 00:48:16.160
suggesting slower aging.

00:48:16.160 --> 00:48:20.640
And, the class of foods associated
with the longest telomeres?

00:48:20.640 --> 00:48:24.800
Vegetarian alternatives: plant-based meat.

00:48:24.800 --> 00:48:30.080
The most important question
in all of nutrition may be,

00:48:30.080 --> 00:48:31.280
“Compared with what?”

00:48:31.280 --> 00:48:36.160
The effects of any given food depend on
what that food is being compared against.

00:48:36.160 --> 00:48:37.760
So, is plant-based meat healthy?

00:48:37.760 --> 00:48:40.080
Compared to the animal products
they were intended to replace?

00:48:40.080 --> 00:48:41.760
Absolutely.

00:48:41.760 --> 00:48:45.760
Plant-based meat alternatives are more
healthful than the meat they replace,

00:48:45.760 --> 00:48:48.640
but perhaps less so than whole plant foods,

00:48:48.640 --> 00:48:50.240
such as legumes and whole grains.

00:48:50.240 --> 00:48:52.815
Wait, just perhaps less so?

00:48:52.960 --> 00:48:55.520
but if we add an even better category,

00:48:55.520 --> 00:48:59.520
whole plant foods do even
better, in many categories.

00:48:59.520 --> 00:49:04.880
For example, bean-based meat averages
five times less saturated fat

00:49:04.880 --> 00:49:06.400
than their meat comparators,

00:49:06.400 --> 00:49:10.400
but actual beans have 40 times less:

00:49:11.840 --> 00:49:16.640
half the trans fat or no trans fat.

00:49:16.640 --> 00:49:19.120
Five grams of fiber is certainly better than zero,

00:49:19.120 --> 00:49:22.720
but nine grams is even better.

00:49:22.720 --> 00:49:26.720
And there are interventional trials
demonstrating the greater benefits.

00:49:26.720 --> 00:49:30.480
For example, better at fighting
insulin resistance and inflammation.

00:49:30.480 --> 00:49:34.240
So certainly, whole foods for the win,

00:49:34.240 --> 00:49:36.240
but plant-based meat alternatives,

00:49:36.240 --> 00:49:39.040
they are not designed to
replace whole plant foods,

00:49:39.040 --> 00:49:43.600
instead to offer a steppingstone
in the transition away from meat.

00:49:43.600 --> 00:49:46.240
I think of it like meat methadone.

00:49:51.120 --> 00:49:53.120
Or, as Professor Gardner put it,

00:49:53.120 --> 00:49:58.480
“I’m hoping that plant-based meats
will be the gateway drug to legumes.”

00:50:01.600 --> 00:50:05.600
This is how the transition to
healthier diets is envisioned,

00:50:05.600 --> 00:50:10.720
from the global burden of disease,
to the global relief of disease,

00:50:10.720 --> 00:50:14.960
as people use plant-based
and mycoprotein-based meats

00:50:14.960 --> 00:50:20.160
to transition toward a more
whole food, plant-based diet.

00:50:20.160 --> 00:50:22.320
With the added saturated fat and sodium,

00:50:22.320 --> 00:50:26.400
plant-based meats are not
healthy—but, they are healthier.

00:50:26.400 --> 00:50:29.200
They are healthier than the foods
they were designed to replace.

00:50:29.200 --> 00:50:32.000
They are better, but not the best.

00:50:32.000 --> 00:50:36.400
Yes, nutrition policies and dietary guidelines,

00:50:36.400 --> 00:50:38.960
the bottom line is that we should
continue to emphasize a diet

00:50:38.960 --> 00:50:40.240
rich in whole plant foods.

00:50:40.240 --> 00:50:44.800
Indeed, if all meat in high income
countries were replaced with whole legumes,

00:50:44.800 --> 00:50:48.720
we could potentially decrease
overall mortality rates by 5 or 6%.

00:50:48.720 --> 00:50:54.080
That means saving 180,000 lives
in the United States every year.

00:50:54.080 --> 00:50:59.680
If instead, we replaced meat with
processed or ultra-processed plants,

00:50:59.680 --> 00:51:04.000
we’d only save about 130,000 Americans a year

00:51:04.000 --> 00:51:06.400
(about a 4% drop in overall mortality).

00:51:06.400 --> 00:51:11.040
That’s still over 100,000 people saved every year.

00:51:11.040 --> 00:51:15.040
So, we shouldn’t let the perfect
be the enemy of the good.

00:51:15.040 --> 00:51:19.520
Plant-based meat alternatives
are better than the alternative.

00:51:19.520 --> 00:51:22.000
Or as one study put it,

00:51:22.000 --> 00:51:27.520
plant-based meat can be
considered “meat with benefits.”

00:51:27.520 --> 00:51:32.560
Thank you so much.

